  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 235 AM EDT Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A warming trend is expected through Thursday with \nmostly dry conditions.\n\nEarly morning analysis shows 1026mb high pressure over the local \narea. Aloft, a broad ridge is in place across the southern third of \nthe CONUS. Skies are mostly clear with just some mid and high level \nclouds note on satellite imagery. Warmer temperatures return to the \narea today as the upper ridge expands eastward into the region. \nAfternoon high temps will top out in the upper 80s to low 90s inland \nwith mid and upper 80s along the coast. Forecast soundings show only \nlimited moisture so staying dry today with some fair wx cumulus \nclouds across the region. Low level winds become southerly today as \nsurface high pressure moves offshore. Moisture will be slow to \nrecover, however. Not as cool tonight with lows in the mid to upper \n60s inland and low 70s for coastal locations. \n\nAn upper trough and surface low well to the north in Canada will \nserve to flatten the upper ridge on Thursday. An associated cold \nfront will approach the region from the NW but will slow as the \nboundary becomes parallel to the mid and upper level zonal flow. \nThursday will be the warmest day of the forecast period with high \ntemps rising into the low/mid 90s. Mid level dry air should allow \nafternoon dew points to mix into the upper 60s and low 70s which \nwill keep a lid on heat index values, generally in the mid to upper \n90s. The latest guidance has trended toward slightly higher chances \nfor isolated convection to sneak into western portions of the area \nThursday afternoon.\n\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...An unsettled pattern develops late Thursday into the \nweekend with daily chances for showers or thunderstorms.\n\n00z models continue to show the slow-moving cold front crossing the \nregion early Friday morning with the boundary likely to stall near \nor just south of the local area. A few showers or storms are \npossible across the south on Friday but some uncertainty remains \nregarding how far south the front will get before stalling and \nlifting back to the north. Not much cold air is forecast behind the \nboundary so high temps on Friday remain above seasonal norms with \nmid 80s N and upper 80s to low 90s S. The front lifts north and \nlingers in the area on Saturday with higher chances for showers and \nstorms across the region. More clouds and precip will result in \nhighs in the low to mid 80s for most of the area with some upper 80s \npossible across the southern third of the CWA. Additional showers \nand storms are possible on Sunday but coverage is expected to be \nlower than Saturday.",
